### What is the Spring AOP proxy and why does it matter?

Spring does not modify your code. Instead, it wraps your bean in a proxy -- an invisible wrapper object that sits between the caller and your real bean. When another bean calls your @Transactional method, the call first hits the proxy. The proxy opens a transaction, calls your real method, and then commits or rolls back. Without the proxy in the middle, no transaction management happens.

Spring creates proxies using CGLIB (the default), which generates a subclass of your bean at runtime. The subclass overrides public methods to add the transactional behavior. This is why Spring AOP only works on public methods called from outside the bean.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

// What Spring actually creates at runtime (simplified)
// SettlementService$$SpringCGLIB$$0 extends SettlementService {
//     @Override
//     public void settle(String tradeId) {
//         begin transaction
//         super.settle(tradeId)   // calls your real method
//         commit transaction
//     }
// }

@Service
public class SettlementService {

    @Transactional
    public void settle(String tradeId) {
        // proxy intercepts this call and manages the transaction
    }
}
```

```text
Call flow with proxy:
Controller -> Proxy.settle() -> begin TX -> Real.settle() -> commit TX

Call flow without proxy (self-invocation):
Real.process() -> this.settle() -> no TX management
```

### Why does @Transactional on a private method do nothing?

Java does not allow a subclass to override a private method. Since CGLIB creates a subclass, it cannot override private methods. The @Transactional annotation is silently ignored. Spring does not warn you, and no transaction is started. This is one of the most dangerous bugs in a banking application because partial data is committed without rollback.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Settlement;
import com.bank.repository.SettlementRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class BrokenSettlementService {

    private final SettlementRepository repo;

    public BrokenSettlementService(SettlementRepository repo) {
        this.repo = repo;
    }

    public void process(Settlement s) {
        persist(s); // self-invocation + private = no proxy, no transaction
    }

    @Transactional  // SILENTLY IGNORED: private method
    private void persist(Settlement s) {
        repo.save(s.getLeg1());
        repo.save(s.getLeg2()); // if this throws, leg1 is NOT rolled back
    }
}
```

```text
Expected: both legs saved or both rolled back.
Actual:   leg1 saved, leg2 fails, leg1 stays in database. Data corruption.
```

### Why does self-invocation bypass the proxy even for public methods?

Even if the method is public, calling it from within the same bean via `this` bypasses the proxy. When Rajesh calls `this.persist(s)`, the call goes directly to the real object, not through the proxy wrapper. The proxy only intercepts calls that come from outside the bean.

The same trap applies to @Async and @Cacheable -- any annotation that relies on the proxy is ignored when called via `this`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Async;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class BrokenNotificationService {

    public void onTransferComplete(String accountId) {
        sendAlert(accountId); // self-invocation: runs synchronously, not async
    }

    @Async  // IGNORED because called via this
    public void sendAlert(String accountId) {
        // This was supposed to run on a background thread
        // but it runs on the request thread, blocking the response
        emailGateway.send(accountId, "Transfer completed for account " + accountId);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected: sendAlert runs on a background thread from the async executor.
Actual:   sendAlert runs synchronously on the request thread.
```

### How do you fix the self-invocation problem?

The clean, preferred fix is to extract the annotated method into a separate @Service bean. This way, the call becomes a genuine external call that passes through the new bean's proxy.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Settlement;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class SettlementService {

    private final SettlementPersister persister; // separate bean

    public SettlementService(SettlementPersister persister) {
        this.persister = persister;
    }

    public void process(Settlement s) {
        persister.persist(s); // external call -> proxy intercepts -> transaction starts
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Settlement;
import com.bank.repository.SettlementRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class SettlementPersister {

    private final SettlementRepository repo;

    public SettlementPersister(SettlementRepository repo) {
        this.repo = repo;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void persist(Settlement s) {
        repo.save(s.getLeg1());
        repo.save(s.getLeg2()); // if this throws, both legs roll back correctly
    }
}
```

An alternative fix is using `AopContext.currentProxy()` to get a reference to the proxy and call through it. This requires `exposeProxy = true` in the configuration and is considered less clean.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.aop.framework.AopContext;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class SettlementServiceWithProxy {

    public void process(String tradeId) {
        // Get the proxy and call through it
        ((SettlementServiceWithProxy) AopContext.currentProxy()).settle(tradeId);
    }

    @Transactional
    public void settle(String tradeId) {
        // now the proxy intercepts this call
    }
}
```

```text
Fix comparison:
- Separate bean (preferred): clean separation, testable, no AOP magic.
- AopContext.currentProxy(): works but couples your code to Spring AOP internals.
- TransactionTemplate (programmatic): wraps code in a transaction without annotations.
```

### How do you propagate context to @Async threads?

SecurityContext, MDC (Mapped Diagnostic Context for logging correlation), and TransactionContext are stored in ThreadLocal. When work jumps to a background thread via @Async, the new thread starts with empty ThreadLocal. The user's identity, the trace ID, and the transaction are all lost.

The fix is a TaskDecorator that copies context from the caller thread to the worker thread.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.slf4j.MDC;
import org.springframework.core.task.TaskDecorator;
import java.util.Map;

public class MdcTaskDecorator implements TaskDecorator {

    @Override
    public Runnable decorate(Runnable runnable) {
        Map<String, String> contextMap = MDC.getCopyOfContextMap();
        return () -> {
            try {
                if (contextMap != null) {
                    MDC.setContextMap(contextMap); // restore MDC on worker thread
                }
                runnable.run();
            } finally {
                MDC.clear(); // clean up after task completes
            }
        };
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.EnableAsync;
import org.springframework.scheduling.concurrent.ThreadPoolTaskExecutor;
import org.springframework.security.task.DelegatingSecurityContextAsyncTaskExecutor;
import java.util.concurrent.Executor;

@Configuration
@EnableAsync
public class AsyncContextConfig {

    @Bean(name = "asyncExecutor")
    public Executor asyncExecutor() {
        ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();
        executor.setCorePoolSize(5);
        executor.setMaxPoolSize(20);
        executor.setQueueCapacity(100);
        executor.setTaskDecorator(new MdcTaskDecorator()); // propagate MDC
        executor.initialize();
        // Wrap with DelegatingSecurityContext to propagate SecurityContext
        return new DelegatingSecurityContextAsyncTaskExecutor(executor);
    }
}
```

```text
Without TaskDecorator:
  Request thread: traceId=abc123, user=rajesh
  Async thread:   traceId=null, user=null  (context lost)

With TaskDecorator:
  Request thread: traceId=abc123, user=rajesh
  Async thread:   traceId=abc123, user=rajesh  (context propagated)
```

### What is JIT compilation and how does it affect performance?

JIT stands for Just-In-Time compilation. The JVM starts by interpreting bytecode, which is slow. When it detects that a method is called frequently (a hot method), the JIT compiler converts that method from bytecode to native machine code so future calls run at hardware speed.

HotSpot JVM uses two compilers: C1 compiles quickly with basic optimizations (good for startup), and C2 compiles slowly but produces highly optimized native code (good for peak throughput). Tiered compilation (the default) uses both: methods start on C1 and get promoted to C2 when they become hot enough.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.RoundingMode;

public class InterestCalculator {

    // This method is called millions of times during batch processing
    // JIT detects it as a hot method and compiles it to native code
    public BigDecimal calculateDailyInterest(BigDecimal principal, BigDecimal annualRate) {
        return principal.multiply(annualRate)
                .divide(BigDecimal.valueOf(365), 6, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
    }
}
```

```bash
# See which methods the JIT compiler compiles
java -XX:+PrintCompilation -jar nbi-account-service.jar

# Example output
# 1234  3  com.bank.service.InterestCalculator::calculateDailyInterest (42 bytes)
# tier 3 = C1, tier 4 = C2
```

### How do sessions work in stateless REST APIs?

Traditional web applications use HttpSession on the server. The server creates a session object, stores user data in it, and sends a session ID cookie to the browser. Every subsequent request carries the cookie, and the server looks up the session.

Stateless REST APIs do not use sessions at all. Instead, authentication is handled by JWT (JSON Web Token). The client sends the JWT in the Authorization header with every request. The server validates the token's signature and extracts the user identity from the claims. No server-side state is stored between requests.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.builders.HttpSecurity;
import org.springframework.security.config.http.SessionCreationPolicy;
import org.springframework.security.web.SecurityFilterChain;

@Configuration
public class StatelessConfig {

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain chain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http.sessionManagement(sm -> sm.sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))
            .oauth2ResourceServer(oauth -> oauth.jwt(jwt -> {}));
        return http.build();
    }
}
```

```text
Session-based (stateful):
  Browser sends: Cookie: JSESSIONID=abc123
  Server looks up session in memory or Redis.

JWT-based (stateless):
  Client sends: Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOi...
  Server validates signature, no session lookup needed.
```

### What are common mistakes with proxy-based annotations?

The most dangerous mistake is putting @Transactional on a private method and assuming it works. Spring does not warn you. The second most common mistake is calling an annotated method from within the same bean.

```java
// WRONG: @Transactional on private method -- silently ignored
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    @Transactional
    private void debit(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // No transaction. Partial writes possible.
    }
}

// WRONG: self-invocation bypasses proxy
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    public void processPayment(String id) {
        this.debit(id, amount); // proxy not involved
    }

    @Transactional
    public void debit(String id, BigDecimal amount) { /* ... */ }
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: separate bean, public method, external call
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    private final PaymentExecutor executor;

    public PaymentService(PaymentExecutor executor) {
        this.executor = executor;
    }

    public void processPayment(String id, BigDecimal amount) {
        executor.debit(id, amount); // external call -> proxy intercepts
    }
}

@Service
public class PaymentExecutor {
    @Transactional
    public void debit(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Transaction is active. Rollback works correctly.
    }
}
```

Another common mistake is forgetting @EnableAsync on the configuration class. Without it, all @Async annotations are completely ignored and methods run synchronously.

### How do you migrate to serverless with Spring Cloud Function?

Spring Cloud Function lets Rajesh write banking logic as a plain Java Function and deploy the same code to AWS Lambda, Azure Functions, or Google Cloud Functions. The function takes an input and returns an output, with no web server.

```java
package com.bank.function;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.RoundingMode;
import java.util.function.Function;

@Configuration
public class LoanEmiFunction {

    @Bean
    public Function<LoanRequest, EmiResponse> calculateEmi() {
        return request -> {
            BigDecimal monthlyRate = request.getAnnualRate()
                    .divide(BigDecimal.valueOf(1200), 10, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
            int months = request.getTenureMonths();
            // EMI = P * r * (1+r)^n / ((1+r)^n - 1)
            BigDecimal factor = monthlyRate.add(BigDecimal.ONE).pow(months);
            BigDecimal emi = request.getPrincipal()
                    .multiply(monthlyRate)
                    .multiply(factor)
                    .divide(factor.subtract(BigDecimal.ONE), 2, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
            return new EmiResponse(request.getAccountId(), emi);
        };
    }
}
```

```text
AWS Lambda input:
{ "accountId": "SAV2001", "principal": 1000000, "annualRate": 8.5, "tenureMonths": 60 }

Lambda output:
{ "accountId": "SAV2001", "emi": 20517.83 }
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **AspectJ weaving vs CGLIB proxies.** CGLIB proxies only intercept external calls to public methods. AspectJ load-time weaving can intercept private methods and self-invocations because it modifies the bytecode directly. Use it when proxy limitations are a real problem, but it adds complexity.
- **TransactionTemplate as an alternative.** When proxy-based @Transactional is awkward, use TransactionTemplate for programmatic transaction management. It wraps a block of code in a transaction without annotations.
- **Never use SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor in production.** The default @Async executor creates a new thread for every task with no upper limit. Configure a bounded ThreadPoolTaskExecutor with core, max, and queue capacity.
- **@TransactionalEventListener.** Use `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)` to fire events only after the transaction commits. This prevents sending notifications for transactions that might roll back.
- **JIT warmup in production.** A freshly started JVM interprets bytecode for the first few thousand calls. In latency-sensitive banking services, use JVM warmup scripts or class data sharing (CDS) to reduce cold-start latency.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Does @Transactional work on a protected method? (Yes with CGLIB, because CGLIB can override protected methods. But Spring documentation recommends using only public methods.)
- What happens if @Async is called from another @Async method? (It works correctly as long as the call goes through the proxy -- meaning it is an external call to a different bean.)
- How do you test that a transaction actually rolls back? (Write an integration test that triggers an exception inside the @Transactional method and assert that no data was persisted.)
- What is the difference between CGLIB and JDK dynamic proxies? (JDK proxies require an interface; CGLIB creates a subclass. Spring Boot defaults to CGLIB since Spring Boot 2.0.)
